import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'AI-Era Defense Overview', href: '/docs/ai-defense' },
  { title: 'Allowlisting', href: '/docs/ai-defense/allowlisting', active: true },
  { title: 'Attack-Surface Hardening', href: '/docs/ai-defense/hardening' },
  { title: 'Deception as a 0-Day Sensor', href: '/docs/ai-defense/deception' },
  { title: 'Capture to Virtual Patch', href: '/docs/ai-defense/response' },
  { title: 'Case Study: Black October', href: '/docs/ai-defense/black-october' },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'why', text: 'Why Allowlisting Beats Signatures', level: 2 },
  { id: 'today', text: 'What Ships Today', level: 2 },
  { id: 'admin', text: 'Admin-Plane Allowlist', level: 2 },
  { id: 'manifest', text: 'Route Manifest (Proposed)', level: 2 },
  { id: 'params', text: 'Parameter Shapes (Proposed)', level: 2 },
  { id: 'learn', text: 'Learning the Manifest', level: 2 },
  { id: 'divert', text: 'Divert, Don’t Block', level: 2 },
  { id: 'honest', text: 'Honest Assessment', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Allowlisting" description="A positive security model: describe what the shop legitimately serves, and treat everything else as suspect without needing to know the vulnerability" section="AI-Era Defense" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <p>
    A signature describes an attack someone has already seen. An allowlist describes your shop, which you
    already know. When frontier models produce new exploits faster than signatures can be written, the
    allowlist is the half of the comparison that stays current.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why">Why Allowlisting Beats Signatures</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Signature (deny) model</th><th>Allowlist (positive) model</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Needs to know the bug</td><td>Yes, one rule per exploit</td><td>No, one rule per legitimate feature</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Size of rule set</td><td>Grows with every CVE ever published</td><td>Grows with the shop's own features</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Novel AI-found exploit</td><td>Missed until someone writes a rule</td><td>Caught if it touches anything outside the manifest</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Who maintains it</td><td>Vendor or community feed</td><td>The shop owner, from their own traffic</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Failure mode</td><td>False negatives (silent)</td><td>False positives (visible, and diverted rather than blocked)</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    Most WordPress exploits, including the ones in the <a href="/docs/detection/cves">seeded CVE table</a>,
    reach a plugin's AJAX action, REST route or PHP file that a normal customer never calls. An allowlist doesn't
    need to know <em>why</em> <code>?mwb_wgm_preview_mail</code> is dangerous. It only needs to know that this
    shop's customers never send it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="today">What Ships Today</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Trusted origins" icon="globe" tag="ip_whitelist" description="CIDR ranges (admin IP, VPN/Tailscale range, office network) get a negative reputation score and are never diverted." meta="ip_rules.lua" />
    <DocCard title="Admin gating" icon="lock" tag="Stage 7" description="wp-admin and login access from an origin not on the allowlist escalates: a warning, then diversion on the third attempt." meta="router.lua" />
    <DocCard title="Known-risky endpoints" icon="shieldAlert" tag="Stages 4–5" description="CVE patterns and direct vulnerable-plugin endpoint access are diverted on the first request." meta="vulnerability_rules.lua" />
  </CardGrid>
  <p>
    These are allowlist-shaped already: the admin plane is trusted <em>by origin</em>, not by guessing whether a
    login is malicious. The proposals below extend the same idea from <em>who</em> may reach the admin plane to
    <em>what</em> may reach the storefront.
  </p>

  <h2 id="admin">Admin-Plane Allowlist</h2>
  <p>This is the cheapest and highest-value step, and it works with the current code. Most AI-found WordPress
  bugs that end in full takeover go through an authenticated or semi-authenticated admin path:
  <code>admin-ajax.php</code> actions missing a capability check, REST routes with a weak
  <code>permission_callback</code>, or <code>wp-admin/*.php</code> includes.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Put every operator behind a fixed origin: a VPN, a Tailscale tailnet (<code>100.64.0.0/10</code>) or an
    office range.</li>
    <li>Add exactly those ranges to <code>threat.ip_whitelist</code> (see
    <a href="/docs/operations/hardening#lua">Hardening → IP Whitelisting</a>), and nothing broader.</li>
    <li>Treat any admin-area request from elsewhere as an attack. Today that means diversion after three attempts.
    A stricter proposed mode (<code>ADMIN_ALLOWLIST_STRICT</code>) diverts on the first one.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="admonition warning">
    <div class="admonition-title">Customer accounts</div>
    <p>WooCommerce customers use <code>/my-account/</code>, not <code>wp-admin</code>. Check that no customer flow
    in your theme redirects through <code>wp-admin</code> or <code>wp-login.php</code> before tightening the
    gate, or legitimate shoppers will be diverted.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="manifest">Route Manifest (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>route manifest</strong> is a declarative list of every request shape the production storefront
    legitimately serves. A request that matches no entry isn't blocked. It is scored, and above the threshold
    it is diverted into a decoy pool exactly like today's CVE matches. The proposal adds it as a new pure module,
    <code>route_manifest_rules.lua</code>, consulted as an early routing stage so it fits the existing
    <a href="/docs/architecture#pipeline">pipeline</a> and pure/adapter split:
  </p>
  <pre><code>-- route_manifest.lua (proposed) — this shop's legitimate surface
return &#123;
  mode = "score",            -- "observe" | "score" | "divert"
  unknown_score = 60,        -- added when nothing matches (threshold is 80)
  routes = &#123;
    &#123; path = "^/$",                 methods = "GET" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/shop/",             methods = "GET" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/product/[%w%-]+/$", methods = "GET" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/cart/$",            methods = "GET POST" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/checkout/$",        methods = "GET POST" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/my%-account/",      methods = "GET POST" &#125;,
    &#123; path = "^/wp%-json/wc/store/", methods = "GET POST" &#125;,  -- block checkout
    &#123; path = "^/wp%-admin/admin%-ajax%.php$", methods = "POST",
      actions = &#123; "woocommerce_add_to_cart", "woocommerce_get_refreshed_fragments" &#125; &#125;,
  &#125;,
&#125;</code></pre>
  <p>Three modes allow a safe rollout:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Mode</th><th>Effect</th><th>Use</th></tr>
    <tr><td><code>observe</code></td><td>Logs <code>route_unlisted</code> security events to the SIEM, with no score change</td><td>First weeks: build and validate the manifest</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>score</code></td><td>Adds <code>unknown_score</code>, so an unlisted request plus any other signal crosses the threshold</td><td>Normal operation</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>divert</code></td><td>An unlisted request is diverted on its own</td><td>High-risk windows, e.g. right after a major plugin advisory</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    The <code>admin-ajax.php</code> entry matters most. WordPress multiplexes hundreds of plugin actions through
    that one file, and it is where much of the AI-found plugin bug class lives. Listing the handful of
    <code>action</code> values the storefront really uses removes every other action from the
    unauthenticated attack surface, including ones from plugins added after the manifest was written.
  </p>

  <h2 id="params">Parameter Shapes (Proposed)</h2>
  <p>Routes alone let an exploit through an allowed endpoint. A second, optional level describes the
  <em>shape</em> of each allowed endpoint's input:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Known parameter names</strong>: an unexpected argument (<code>file[path]</code>,
    <code>integration_id</code>, <code>supported_type</code>) adds score even on an allowed route.</li>
    <li><strong>Type and length bounds</strong>: <code>product_id</code> is digits, <code>quantity</code> is 1–3
    digits, a coupon code is ≤ 32 word characters. Out-of-shape values are what injection payloads look like,
    whatever the bug.</li>
    <li><strong>Content types</strong>: storefront routes accept form or JSON bodies only. A
    <code>multipart/form-data</code> body outside the upload routes you list is diverted, which covers the
    file-upload RCE class without knowing the plugin. This extends today's
    <a href="/docs/detection/capabilities"><code>upload_rules.lua</code></a>. It is also the main pre-patch
    control for parser-level bugs like <a href="/docs/ai-defense/black-october#guard">PHP2Shell</a>, whose
    triggers are all multipart.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="learn">Learning the Manifest</h2>
  <p>Nobody should write a manifest by hand from memory. The project already records every request in the SIEM,
  so the manifest can be <em>derived</em>:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Run in <code>observe</code> mode for a representative period, including a sales peak if possible.</li>
    <li>A proposed dashboard tool, <strong>Detection Policy → Route Manifest</strong>, aggregates production-routed
    requests from sessions that never crossed any threshold. It groups them by normalised path, method and
    parameter names, and proposes entries above a minimum hit count.</li>
    <li>The operator reviews each proposed entry. Anything unexpected (a plugin endpoint you didn't know was
    public) is a finding in itself: it's either a feature to keep or surface to remove (see
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/hardening">Hardening</a>).</li>
    <li>Commit the manifest next to <code>init.lua</code>, switch to <code>score</code>, and watch
    <code>route_unlisted</code> events in Kibana for false positives.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Learning from <em>your own</em> traffic is the key difference from defensive AI. The manifest isn't a
  prediction about attackers. It's a record of customers, which an attacker can't shift by being smarter.</p>

  <h2 id="divert">Divert, Don’t Block</h2>
  <p>A conventional WAF answers an unlisted request with a 403. That has two costs in the AI era:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It's an oracle.</strong> An agent iterating on an exploit learns from every block and mutates
    until something passes. A diverted request gets a normal-looking shop back, so there's no gradient to
    follow.</li>
    <li><strong>It throws the exploit away.</strong> A blocked 0-day is a log line. A diverted one runs in a
    cloned pool, where the full exploit chain, payloads and post-exploitation steps are recorded (see
    <a href="/docs/ai-defense/deception">Deception</a>).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Because a false positive is diverted, not refused, a too-tight manifest degrades to "a real customer
  browses a believable clone" rather than "a real customer sees an error". The device signature and decay
  policy then let that customer recover, so a strict allowlist is far less risky here than in a blocking
  WAF.</p>

  <h2 id="honest">Honest Assessment</h2>
  <p><strong>Strengths:</strong> Works against bugs nobody has found yet. Derived from the shop's own traffic.
  Explainable entry by entry. Fails toward diversion rather than outage. The admin-plane allowlist works with
  current code.</p>
  <p><strong>Limitations:</strong> The route manifest and parameter shapes are proposals, not shipped code. An
  exploit using an allowed route with in-shape parameters (for example a logic bug in checkout) isn't caught by
  this layer. Diversion of a legitimate customer mid-checkout is a real business cost, so roll out in
  <code>observe</code> mode first. The manifest needs updating whenever plugins add public features.</p>
</DocsLayout>
